Answer key (with brief explanations)
1) B — Static NAT maps one private IP to one public IP permanently.
2) C — PAT (port address translation, overload) lets many private hosts share one public IP using port numbers.
3) B — Use "ip nat inside" on the interface to mark it as NAT inside.
4) B — Stratum 1 are devices directly attached to a reference clock; Stratum 0 refers to the physical reference clocks themselves.
5) B — DHCP follows Discover → Offer → Request → Acknowledge.
6) C — PTR records map IP addresses to hostnames for reverse lookups.
7) C — SNMPv3 supports authentication and encryption (authPriv); v1/v2c use community strings.
8) C — Informational messages are level 6 (0 = emergency ... 7 = debugging).
9) B — Shaping buffers and smooths traffic to a rate; policing drops or remarks excess immediately.
10) A — A hostname and domain name are required before generating RSA keys (crypto key generate rsa) for SSH.





