## 4. SNMP

What: Monitors and manages devices. A manager (NMS) polls or receives alerts from an agent on each device, which exposes data in a MIB identified by OIDs.

Analogy: a supervisor walking around asking each machine for its status (polling), while a machine can ring an alarm bell on its own (trap). The MIB is the machine's list of gauges, and an OID is one gauge's address.

Ports: UDP 161 (manager queries the agent), UDP 162 (agent sends traps to the manager).

Messages:

| Message | Direction | Note |
|---|---|---|
| Get / GetNext / GetBulk | Manager → agent | Read; GetBulk is v2c and v3 |
| Set | Manager → agent | Write |
| Response | Agent → manager | Reply to a request |
| Trap | Agent → manager | Unacknowledged |
| Inform | Agent → manager | Acknowledged (v2c, v3) |

Versions:

| Version | Security |
|---|---|
| v1 | Community string, cleartext |
| v2c | Community string, cleartext; adds GetBulk, Inform, 64-bit counters |
| v3 | Authentication and encryption; users, groups, views |

v3 security levels: noAuthNoPriv, authNoPriv, authPriv (auth and encryption, the recommended level).

Configuration:
```
! v2c
snmp-server community READONLY ro
snmp-server host 10.1.1.100 version 2c READONLY
snmp-server enable traps

! v3
snmp-server group ADMINS v3 priv
snmp-server user snmpuser ADMINS v3 auth sha AuthPass123 priv aes 128 PrivPass123
```

Verify: `show snmp`, `show snmp community`, `show snmp user`.

Traps:
- Never leave default community strings such as `public` or `private`.
- Use v3 authPriv wherever possible.
- `rw` communities allow configuration changes, so avoid them.

