## 5. Syslog

What: Standard logging. Devices send messages to a central server over UDP 514.

Analogy: a ship's logbook where each entry carries a severity tag, with a copy sent to head office.

Severity levels (lower number = more severe):

| Level | Name |
|---|---|
| 0 | Emergency |
| 1 | Alert |
| 2 | Critical |
| 3 | Error |
| 4 | Warning |
| 5 | Notice |
| 6 | Informational |
| 7 | Debugging |

Mnemonic: Every Awesome Cisco Engineer Will Need Icecream Daily.

Rule: setting a level logs that level and all more severe ones (lower numbers). `logging trap 4` sends levels 0-4.

Message format:
```
%LINK-3-UPDOWN: Interface GigabitEthernet0/1, changed state to up
%FACILITY-SEVERITY-MNEMONIC: description
```

Destinations and defaults:

| Destination | Command | Default level |
|---|---|---|
| Console | `logging console` | 7 (debugging) |
| Buffer (RAM) | `logging buffered` | 7 (debugging) |
| VTY sessions | `terminal monitor` | 7 (debugging) |
| Syslog server | `logging host` / `logging trap` | 6 (informational) |

Configuration:
```
logging host 10.1.1.50
logging trap warnings
logging source-interface loopback0
logging buffered 16384 informational
service timestamps log datetime msec
service sequence-numbers
```

Facility: local0 to local7 for forwarding to a server. Cisco default is local7.

Verify: `show logging`.

Traps:
- Remote syslog is UDP, unacknowledged, so messages can be lost.
- Without NTP, timestamps from different devices can't be correlated.
- Debug level on the console can overload a router.

