## 8. TFTP and FTP

Analogy:
- TFTP is a package left in an unlocked mailbox. There is no ID check.
- FTP is a courier who checks ID, but your password is shouted across the hallway.
- SFTP and SCP are armored delivery.

| | TFTP | FTP | SFTP / SCP |
|---|---|---|---|
| Transport | UDP | TCP | TCP (over SSH) |
| Port | 69 | 20 (data), 21 (control) | 22 |
| Authentication | None | Username/password | Username/password or keys |
| Encryption | None | None (cleartext) | Yes |
| Typical use | IOS image or config backup, phone config, PXE boot | File transfer with directory support | Secure device file transfers |

Cisco commands:
```
copy running-config tftp:
copy tftp: flash:
copy startup-config ftp:
ip ftp username ADMIN
ip ftp password SECRET
copy flash:image.bin scp:
verify /md5 flash:image.bin       ! confirm the image is intact
```
Check space with `show flash:`.

Traps:
- TFTP has no authentication and no encryption, so use it only on trusted segments.
- FTP sends credentials in cleartext. Prefer SCP or SFTP in production.
- Always verify an IOS image (MD5 or SHA) before reloading.

